Running the Project
Step 1: Download Files

Download all project files from the GitHub repository.

Step 2: Open Notebook

Launch Jupyter Notebook:

jupyter notebook


Open:

Facebook_Analysis.ipynb

Step 3: Load Dataset

Verify that the dataset file is located in the same directory as the notebook.

Example:

df = pd.read_csv("dataset.csv")


If using Google Colab:

from google.colab import files
uploaded = files.upload()

Step 4: Execute the Notebook

Run cells from top to bottom using:

Run All


or

Shift + Enter


for individual cells.

The notebook must be executed sequentially because later sections depend on variables created earlier.

Data Preparation

The notebook performs several preprocessing tasks:

Missing Values

Missing observations are identified and handled before model creation.

Variable Transformation

Categorical variables are converted into dummy variables for use in regression models.

Examples include:

Post Type
Category
Time Period
Feature Engineering

Additional variables may be created such as:

Morning
Afternoon
Evening

These features allow the model to evaluate posting time effects.

Multiple Linear Regression
Purpose

Predict the amount of engagement a post will receive.

Model Output

The regression model provides:

Coefficients
p-values
R²
Confidence intervals
Interpretation

Positive coefficients indicate engagement tends to increase when the variable increases.

Negative coefficients indicate engagement tends to decrease when the variable increases.

Variables with:

p-value < 0.05


are considered statistically significant.

Logistic Regression
Purpose

Predict whether a post will have:

High Engagement


or

Low Engagement


based on a chosen engagement threshold.

Model Output

The model provides:

Odds ratios
Coefficients
Accuracy
Classification metrics
Interpretation

Odds Ratio > 1:

Higher likelihood of high engagement


Odds Ratio < 1:

Lower likelihood of high engagement

Model Evaluation

The notebook reports performance metrics such as:

Linear Regression
R²
Adjusted R²
Residual analysis
Logistic Regression
Accuracy
Precision
Recall
F1 Score
Confusion Matrix

These metrics help determine how effectively the models explain and predict engagement outcomes.

Making Predictions

To generate a new prediction:

Update input values for a Facebook post.
Run the prediction cell.
Review generated engagement estimates.

Example:

new_post = pd.DataFrame({
    'Paid':[1],
    'Type_Video':[1],
    'Month':[7]
})

prediction = model.predict(new_post)
print(prediction)


The output will represent the predicted engagement level for that post.

Understanding the Results
Significant Findings

The analysis identifies which factors most strongly influence engagement.

Common findings may include:

Certain post types outperform others.
Paid promotions impact engagement.
Timing can affect interaction rates.
Content characteristics influence user response.
Troubleshooting
File Not Found Error

Verify:

pd.read_csv("dataset.csv")


matches the actual dataset filename.

Module Not Found Error

Install missing packages:

pip install package_name

Model Errors

Check that:

Data preprocessing cells were executed.
Dummy variables were created correctly.
Missing values were handled.
Reproducing Results

To reproduce the exact results:

Use the provided dataset.
Run all cells in order.
Do not modify preprocessing steps.
Use the same random seed values specified in the notebook.

Following these steps will generate the same statistical results, visualizations, and business recommendations presented in the project report.

